# Vite Asset Import Best Practices

## The Problem

Using absolute paths for assets in Vite projects prevents proper bundling:

```typescript
// ❌ Wrong - Won't be bundled
const imagePath = "/src/assets/image.png";
<img src="/src/assets/image.png" />;
```

## The Solution

Always use ES module imports for assets to ensure proper bundling:

```typescript
// ✅ Correct - Will be bundled
import imageSrc from "../assets/image.png";
<img :src="imageSrc" />
```

## Key Points

- Vite only bundles assets that are imported as ES modules
- Absolute paths are treated as external URLs, not local assets
- Use `import` statements for all assets (images, icons, etc.)
- Bind imported assets to template attributes with `:src`, `:image`, etc.

## Common Patterns

```typescript
// In Vue components
import logo from "../assets/logo.png";
import character from "../assets/character/happy.png";

// In constants/utilities
import assetSrc from "./assets/image.png";
export const ASSET_PATH = assetSrc;
```

This ensures all referenced assets are properly processed, optimized, and included in the build output.

